target/
.claude/
.serena/
.planning/
# hiss's own lockfile stays untracked: the Downstream gate exists to prove the
# published requirement ranges resolve fresh, the way a consumer resolves them.
Cargo.lock
# The out-of-workspace crates are the opposite case — unpublished, no consumers
# to resolve them, and `hiss-interop`'s comparison benchmark is only comparable
# across time if snow's version holds still. `demo/Cargo.lock` is tracked for
# the same reason; the scheduled Interop run does `cargo update` first so the
# pin never hides an upstream release.
!hiss-interop/Cargo.lock
# `hiss-aesgcm-lab` has the strongest claim of the three: it exists to test one
# frozen upstream cryptoxide commit, and a `rev`-pinned git dependency without
# a committed lock re-resolves its transitive graph freely. Its scheduled run
# deliberately does NOT `cargo update` — that would defeat the pin, which is
# the whole point of the crate.
!hiss-aesgcm-lab/Cargo.lock
